p to  monitor and encourage the implementation  of the recommendations contained in that  report of course the activities of  private professional organizations that  set standards and provide gardens are  not the entire answer to effective  disclosure policy oversight  unaccompanied by company and transaction  by transaction basis the Commission has  a responsibility to ensure the fair  presentation of the substance of  transactions and the financial results  of entities this function is most  crucial for new transactions for novel  financial instruments and for economic  difficulties in recognition of the  importance of fair presentation the  division of corporation finance is  substantially increasing the number of  accountants on its staff as I've as I  indicated earlier our goal is to have  accountants comprised half of the  professional staff of the operating  branches of that division the increase  in the accounting staff will enable the  division to better address the  increasing frequency of sophisticated  complex and novel accounting issues as  well as to review more issuers financial  statements as I've indicated earlier  financial disclosure is the heart of our  disclosure system and it is the  accountants viewpoints in the  accountants presentations the  accountants accuracy which preserves  those statements for the public in  addition to encouraging the accounting  profession to improve financial  disclosure and to strengthening its own  review process and the division of  corporation finance the Commission is  also enhancing its enforcement process  the Commission devote substantial  resource in its resources and its  division of enforcement to uncovering  and prosecuting instances involving  accounting and financial reporting  abuses when the Commission staff  discovers instances of financial fraud  or other abuses by registrant's it also  examines the role of the auditor to  determine whether the auditor has aided  and abetted issuer violations of the  federal securities laws or engaged in  improper professional conduct over the  past few years the committee  has brought an increased number of cases  against both issuers and their  accountants for failures in the  financial disclosure process these cases  will be discussed by others in the  program today so I won't dwell on them  here I would note however that a number  of these cases emphasize the  significance of auditor independence as  a means of preventing fraud and other  misconduct I consider these areas to be  a particular importance since they  emphasize to the profession there where  auditors fail in their role the  Commission will take vigorous  enforcement action although I've been  discussing enforcement of the  enhancement of the accountants role in  disclosure I want to add a word about an  area which will affect the accounting  profession for years to come the  internationalization of the securities  markets poses significant challenges to  this country's financial disclosure  system challenges that inevitably will  involve the accounting profession as  foreign issuers expand their use of US  capital markets and domestic issuers  seek seek access to foreign markets our  capital markets are and will be  undergoing a significant change  and who night couldn't give me in 1986  for instance foreign purchases of US  stocks reached 148 billion and US  purchases of foreign stocks totaled 51  billion as compared with 82 and 25  billion in 1985 in addition there has  been a large surge in multi national  debt and equity offerings for example  between 1980 and 1986 offerings in the  international bond market including the  Euro bond markets grew from 38 billion  to 227 billion international bond  trading volume in 1986 was more than 3.5  trillion dollars and total international  bond offerings equaled 102 billion in  the first half of 1987 international  equity market says they also shown  remarkable growth euro equity offerings  grew from 220 million dollars in 1983 to  almost 12 billion dollars in 1986 and in  the first half of 1987 total seven and  one half billion there is no doubt that  this internationalization of the markets  brings great benefits to issuers and  investors but it also presents difficult  new challenges foremost among these  challenges is the development of  adequate disclosure standards given the  importance of financial disclosure a key  element of international disclosure  still the standards will be to develop  mutually acceptable international  accounting and auditing standards the  Commission already has a number of  important steps underway to facilitate  this process since 1982 for example the  Commission has had a separate integrated  disclosure system for offerings of  foreign securities registered under the  Securities Act under this system  financial statements do not have to be  prepared in accordance with us gaap if  they comply with gap in the home country  as long as the net income and balance  sheet totals are reconciled to what they  would have been if US requirements have  been followed additionally the  Commission staff is developing a  proposal with other countries for  reciprocal disclosure for the  registration of certain  supplied security the goal is to allow  the offering document from an issuer's  home country to be used as the  prospectus for offerings in the United  States one of the difficult reciprocity  reciprocity issue is being examined by  the staff concerns financial statements  since accounting principles auditing  standards and auditor independence are  critical to the integrity and  credibility of the u.s. disclosure  system it is also essential that any  cooperative approach emphasized the  importance of these well-established  standard setting systems in financial  statement statements regarding  accounting standards an important  objective in the international area is  the identification of reliable  reciprocal accounting standards true  reciprocity would allow an issuer to  prepare only one set of disclosure  documents for worldwide use and would  not have a reconciliation requirement we  hope that continued development and  refinement of international accounting  standards will result in movement  towards similar accounting standards and  will lessen the need for country to  country reconciliation in contrast to  accounting standards auditing standards  differences are not susceptible to  accommodation through reconciliation we  have urged compliance with us gas and we  believe progress in the development of  international auditing standards has  taken place we are encouraged that these  standards may be relied upon for  reciprocity in the future the Commission  will continue to work with international  organizations such as the International  Accounting Standards Committee the  International Federation of accountants  and the international organization of  securities commissions and similars  agent agencies in order to further  efforts to bring about greater  reconciliation and reciprocity I  encourage your participation in these  efforts I would like to make one  statement about the international area  we are in the process that the  commission of developing report on the  market events of October that mark that  market study will include a section  on the relationship of the US markets to  international markets and as one reviews  the events of those a market of that  market crash in October and looks at the  systems which are now in place including  or order routing systems systems which  provide quotation information and last  price information one sees and  automation of the entire securities  markets which will I think inevitably  lead to a greater internationalization  of the markets and what we are going to  see I'm sure is the trading of many US  securities in overseas markets and  efforts to have overseas securities  traded in in our markets this this  progress will occur and the job of the  Securities and Exchange Commission with  the help of the accounting profession  will be to see that the disclosures in  these financial markets are in  accordance as closely as possible with  our standard I believe that our  financial disclosure standards and the  efforts of the accounting profession in  in helping to achieve those standards  has been a one strong and fundamental  reason for the development of fine  capital markets in the United States and  I'm hoping that by by persuasion and  example we in the United States can  increase the amount of disclosure which  occurs in other markets and contribute  to sound stable and honest international  markets may I conclude my remarks by  emphasizing the significance of the  accounting profession to the economy of  the United States our economy depends on  capital markets and those markets depend  on a first-class financial disclosure  system as auditors and accountants you  are responsible for the credibility of  the financial disclosure system the  Commission has been a partner with your  profession for over 50 years and  partnership has worked well as with  every partnership however effort must be  invested to assure that the terms of the  partnership are fulfilled the Commission  will continue to monitor the professions  activities and will proceed through  oversight rulemaking and enforcement  action where necessary to seek excellent  in our nation's full disclosure system  thank you
 

